"The concepts of bid'a and bid'at refer to unprecedented new things. With the verse, 'Say: I am not the first of the messengers,' the Prophet (peace be upon him) states that he did not introduce an innovation different from those before him, but rather followed their path. Furthermore, a witness from the Children of Israel saw the likeness of Prophet Muhammad in the Torah and believed. It is narrated that this witness was Abdullah ibn Salam, though some say it was Moses (Musa). There is a difference of opinion as to whether the verse is Makki or Madani.\n\nIn verse 11, it is reported that the disbelievers belittled the believers, saying, 'If there were any good in Islam, they would not have preceded us to it,' and then would say, 'This is an ancient lie.' These words were spoken by both the polytheists and the Jews. Most of the believers were poor people like Ammar, Suhayb, and Bilal; by disparaging them, they sought to belittle Islam.\n\nIn verse 12, it is emphasized that before the Quran, the Torah, the book of Moses, was a guide and a mercy. The Quran, on the other hand, is a book revealed in Arabic, confirming the previous scriptures, warning the wrongdoers, and giving glad tidings to the doers of good. In verses 13-14, it is stated that there will be no fear or grief for those who say, 'Our Lord is Allah,' and remain steadfast, and that they are the companions of Paradise.\n\nIn verse 15, Allah enjoins man to do good to his parents. The hardships endured by the mother are described: the period of pregnancy and weaning is thirty months. This period is interpreted as six months of pregnancy and two years of nursing. An incident that took place between Umar and Ali indicates that the minimum duration of pregnancy is six months. When a person reaches the age of forty, he prays to give thanks to Allah, to perform righteous deeds, and for the rectification of his offspring. This supplication was revealed concerning Abu Bakr.\n\nIn verse 16, glad tidings are given that the best of what these righteous people have done will be accepted and their misdeeds will be overlooked. The expression 'the best' encompasses acts of obedience such as the recommended (mandub) and the obligatory (wajib). In the counsel Abu Bakr gave to Umar, it is explained that Allah has rights by night and by day, and that the heaviness of the Scale (mizan) depends on following the truth.\n\nIn verses 17-18, a contrasting example is presented: a person who says 'Fie upon you' to his parents and denies the resurrection. His parents, seeking refuge in Allah, call him to faith, but he says, 'This is nothing but legends of the ancients.' Such are those among the communities of jinn and men that passed away before them against whom the decree of punishment has been realized; they are the ruined. In verses 19-20, it is stated that everyone has ranks according to what they have done, and that because the disbelievers indulged in the pleasure of good things in this world, they will face a humiliating punishment in the Hereafter.\n\nIn verse 20, it is explained that the disbelievers who exhausted their good things in the life of this world will receive no reward in the Hereafter. It is narrated that upon this verse, Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) did not become attached to worldly pleasures, wishing to preserve his good deeds for the Hereafter. Furthermore, the poverty of the People of the Bench (Ahl al-Suffah) is related, along with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) stating that their condition was better for them in the world.\n\nIn verses 21-23, it is recounted that Hud (peace be upon him), the prophet of the people of 'Ad, warned his people in the region of al-Ahqaf. Ahqaf means curved sand dunes and points to the frailty of their land despite the might of the people of 'Ad. Hud told them not to worship anything other than Allah and stated that he feared for them the punishment of a tremendous day.\n\nIn verses 24-25, it is related that the punishment approached in the form of a cloud; the people rejoiced, thinking it was a rain cloud, but it was a wind bearing a painful punishment. The wind demolished everything and destroyed them. Narrated from Ibn Abbas, it is stated that the wind submerged the people in a torrent of sand for seven nights and eight days, ultimately casting them into the sea. Hud and the believers were spared.\n\nIn verses 26-28, it is emphasized that the faculties Allah bestowed upon the people of 'Ad (hearing, sight, intellect/hearts) availed them nothing because they rejected the signs of Allah. The destroyed lands around them (such as Ma'rib, Thamud, and Sodom) are cited as examples. It is explained that the deities they had taken besides Allah did not help them; on the contrary, they vanished from them.\n\nIn verses 29-32, it is related that a group of jinn was directed to the Prophet (peace be upon him) to listen to the Quran. After listening to the Quran, these jinn returned to their people and called them to faith. They said that the scripture revealed after Moses guided to the right path, that those who respond to Allah's caller will be forgiven, and that those who do not respond cannot escape Allah.\n\nIn verses 33-35, it is stated that Allah, Who is able to give life to the dead, did not grow weary with creating the heavens and the earth. It is reported that on the day the disbelievers are exposed to the Fire, they will confess the truth. The Prophet is commanded to be patient just as the messengers of steadfast resolve (ulu al-'azm) were patient. It is stated that when they witness the punishment of the Hereafter, the time they remained in the world will seem short to them, and that none will be destroyed except the defiantly disobedient (fasiqun)."